    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ce7473c0-3b7c-578d-b492-1ec77ac71f56",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186129154/plots/b5c8c1763e64c00d2ae1fe9867d9a0211d976ab1cbdc47ef8666e687a6d84c68.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $x=-5$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n\nII. $x=1$ is **not** a simple root.\n\nIII. $x=1$ is a root of even multiplicity.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The root $x=-5$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=-5$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=1$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at that point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=1$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=1$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
